module immunoassayDetection

manifest AntiHumanIgG_Capture
manifest HumanIgG_Sample
manifest FITC_AntiHumanIgG_Detection
manifest PluronicF127_RinseBuffer
manifest BovineSerum_Spiked

// ============================================================
// A Digital Microfluidic Approach to Heterogeneous Immunoassays
// Miller, Ng, Uddayasankar, Wheeler - Anal. Bioanal. Chem. (2011)
// DOI: 10.1007/s00216-010-4368-2
//
// Protocol: Heterogeneous sandwich immunoassay for Human IgG
// on a DMF device. Capture antibody (Fc-specific anti-human IgG)
// is pre-spotted (physisorbed) on the Cytop/ITO top plate.
// All washing and incubation steps are performed via DMF
// actuation at 75 V_rms, 18 kHz.
//
// DEVICE CONFIGURATION:
//   Bottom plate: patterned Cr electrodes, 1x1 mm actuation
//     electrodes + 2.5x2.5 mm reservoir electrodes.
//     Coated with 2 um parylene C + 130 nm Cytop.
//   Top plate: unpatterned ITO/glass, 130 nm Cytop.
//     Pre-spotted with capture antibody (0.22-2.2 mg/mL,
//     250-500 nL per spot, air-dried, rinsed with 0.1% w/v
//     Pluronic F-127 in DI water, dried before use).
//   Gap spacer: ~150 um (two pieces double-sided tape).
//   Droplet volume: 1 uL unit droplets.
//   Driving potential: 75 V_rms, 18 kHz function generator.
//   Surroundings: air (no carrier oil; Pluronic prevents NSB).
//   Detection: PheraStar plate reader, lambda_ex=485 nm,
//     lambda_em=520 nm, focal height=12.1 mm.
//
// REAGENT NOTES:
//   All solutions contain 0.00625% (w/v) Pluronic F-127
//   (except diluted serum: 0.0113% Pluronic F-127) to
//   prevent non-specific protein adsorption to device surfaces
//   and to facilitate DMF droplet movement. This eliminates
//   the need for BSA blocking steps used in well-plate methods.
//   All solutions buffered in 10 mM phosphate buffer pH 7.4.
//   Pluronic F-127 is included in the rinse buffer manifest.
//
// ASSAY SUMMARY (sandwich format):
//   1. Sample droplet (antigen) cycled 10x over capture spot
//   2. Rinse buffer droplet (2 passes) removes non-specific IgG
//   3. Detection antibody droplet cycled 10x over spot
//   4. Final rinse buffer droplet (2 passes)
//   5. Fluorescence readout on plate reader
//
// TIMING:
//   Conventional 96-well plate: up to 2 days (12 h antibody
//     immobilization + 2-12 h BSA blocking + 1-2 h per
//     binding step).
//   DMF assay: 2.5 h total from spot printing to completion.
//   Diffusion distances ~100x smaller + active droplet transport
//   eliminates diffusion-limited kinetics.
//
// THROUGHPUT:
//   Multiplexed devices: 6-10 platforms wired in series,
//   electrodes bussed together for simultaneous parallel
//   control of replicate assays. This .bs file encodes a
//   single-platform assay; replicate platforms share identical
//   protocol steps driven simultaneously.
//
// WASTE REDUCTION:
//   Well plate: ~3.5 mL per well.
//   DMF: ~4 uL per assay spot (reduction of ~3 orders of
//   magnitude).
//
// VALIDATION:
//   Analyte: Human IgG (0-5 ng total mass on device).
//   Matrix tested: bovine serum diluted 100x, spiked with
//     human IgG (0-10 ug/mL). Bovine IgG at ~100 ug/mL
//     (100-fold excess over highest human IgG doped). No
//     significant cross-reactivity observed.
//   Dynamic range: ~1 order of magnitude (R^2=0.9964 on DMF).
//   Reproducibility: %RSD < 25% per data point (3-5 replicates).
//
// FIX (v2): captureSpot is never disposed -- it models the
//   physical antibody spot immobilized on the ITO top plate,
//   which persists throughout all assay steps. In v1,
//   antigenBound was disposed after STEP 3 but then reused
//   in STEP 4 (rinse) and STEP 6 (detection antibody), causing
//   a use-after-dispose in BioScript's data-flow model.
//   Fix: all downstream mix steps reference captureSpot
//   directly. Only liquid droplets (antigenBound,
//   rinseStep1, sandwichComplex, rinseStep2) are disposed.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main              -- sample incubation, rinse 1,
//                             detection antibody incubation,
//                             rinse 2, fluorescence detection
//   BB2  assayPass         -- assaySignal > 0: valid FITC signal
//                             detected; dispose final droplet
//   BB3  assayFail         -- assaySignal <= 0: no signal;
//                             drain final droplet (failed assay)
//   BB4  finalCleanup      -- terminal: dispose QC signal;
//                             experiment ends
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PRE-CONDITION: Capture antibody spot already immobilized on
//   Cytop/ITO top plate before device assembly.
//   Fc-specific anti-human IgG spotted at 0.22-2.2 mg/mL,
//   250-500 nL. Air-dried, rinsed with 0.1% Pluronic F-127,
//   dried. Top plate assembled onto bottom plate with spacer.
//   Reservoirs loaded with 1-uL aliquots of antigen sample
//   and rinse buffer before assembly.
//
// NOTE ON CYTOP vs TEFLON-AF:
//   Teflon-AF (50 nm, standard DMF coating) was found to be
//   too fragile for surface-based assays -- antibody printing
//   caused tears that prevented actuation. Cytop (130 nm)
//   was used instead for its greater mechanical ruggedness,
//   allowing spot drying and rinsing without delamination.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Dispense antigen sample droplet.
//   Human IgG in 10 mM phosphate buffer pH 7.4 with 0.00625%
//   Pluronic F-127. Concentrations tested: 0-1 ug/mL (on-chip
//   volume 1 uL = 0-1 ng per assay; standard curve range
//   0-5 ng total IgG per spot as reported in Fig. 4).
//   For serum experiments: bovine serum diluted 100x, spiked
//   with human IgG 0-10 ug/mL (serum uses 0.0113% Pluronic).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

sampleDroplet = dispense 1 units of HumanIgG_Sample

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Dispense capture spot (surface-bound antibody).
//   captureSpot models the pre-immobilized Fc-specific
//   anti-human IgG spot on the Cytop/ITO top plate.
//   This variable is NEVER disposed -- it represents a
//   physical surface feature that persists for all assay
//   steps, not a liquid droplet consumed by actuation.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

captureSpot = dispense 1 units of AntiHumanIgG_Capture

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Sample incubation - antigen binding to capture spot.
//   DMF actuation (75 V_rms, 18 kHz) translates the 1-uL
//   sample droplet across the capture antibody spot and cycles
//   it back and forth 10 times (Fig. 2a/b).
//   Active transport eliminates diffusion-limited kinetics:
//   diffusion distances are ~100x smaller than in well plates,
//   and droplet motion continually replenishes antigen at the
//   surface. This reduces binding time dramatically relative
//   to static well-plate incubation (1-2 h per step).
//   NOTE: `mix` abstracts 10-pass cyclic actuation across
//   the capture antibody spot on the ITO top plate.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

antigenBound = mix sampleDroplet with captureSpot for 10m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Sample removal.
//   Sample droplet withdrawn to reservoir via DMF actuation.
//   Waste droplet (containing unbound IgG and matrix proteins)
//   manually removed from chip with a laboratory tissue (Fig. 2b).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

dispose antigenBound

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: First rinse - remove non-specifically bound IgG.
//   1-uL rinse buffer droplet (10 mM phosphate buffer pH 7.4,
//   0.00625% Pluronic F-127) is actuated across the spot in
//   2 passes to wash away non-specifically adsorbed proteins.
//   Two passes shown in Fig. 3 (inset) to be sufficient for
//   removing NSB while retaining specific binding.
//   Rinse droplet removed to waste reservoir after completion.
//   NOTE: `mix` abstracts 2-pass rinse actuation.
//   References captureSpot (persistent surface), not the
//   disposed antigenBound droplet variable.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

rinseBuffer1 = dispense 1 units of PluronicF127_RinseBuffer
rinseStep1   = mix rinseBuffer1 with captureSpot for 2m

dispose rinseStep1

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Device reload for detection antibody.
//   After removing sample waste and rinse waste, the device
//   top plate (Cytop/ITO with capture spot) is replaced and
//   the bottom plate reservoirs are reloaded with 1-uL
//   aliquots of FITC-anti-human IgG (Fab-specific, raised
//   in goat) and rinse buffer (Fig. 2c).
//   The detection antibody solution contains 0.00625% Pluronic.
//
// NOTE ON DETECTION ANTIBODY BINDING (from Fig. 3):
//   Binding curve shows saturation after only 2 passes of the
//   detection antibody droplet across the antigen spot.
//   10 passes are used in the optimized protocol to eliminate
//   variance from fluid movement timing. Pluronic in the droplet
//   does not strip previously bound antibody during subsequent
//   passes, confirming binding stability.
//   Reynolds number ~210 (laminar flow); spot captures antibody
//   from the layer in direct contact with the surface per pass.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

detectionAntibody = dispense 1 units of FITC_AntiHumanIgG_Detection

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: Detection antibody incubation.
//   DMF delivers the FITC-labeled detection antibody (Fab-
//   specific anti-human IgG) to the assay spot and cycles it
//   back and forth 10 times (Fig. 2c/d).
//   The detection antibody binds the antigen (human IgG)
//   already captured on the spot, completing the sandwich.
//   FITC label: excitation 485 nm, emission 520 nm.
//   NOTE: `mix` abstracts 10-pass cyclic actuation across
//   the assay spot for detection antibody binding.
//   References captureSpot (persistent surface).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

sandwichComplex = mix detectionAntibody with captureSpot for 10m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: Detection antibody removal.
//   Detection antibody droplet removed to waste reservoir.
//   Manually cleared with laboratory tissue (Fig. 2d).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

dispose sandwichComplex

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9: Final rinse - remove unbound detection antibody.
//   1-uL rinse buffer droplet cycled across the spot in
//   2 passes to remove any remaining unbound FITC-anti-IgG
//   (Fig. 2e). Rinse droplet removed to waste reservoir
//   (Fig. 2f). This final rinse is critical: residual
//   detection antibody in the droplet would generate high
//   background fluorescence in the plate reader step.
//   NOTE: `mix` abstracts 2-pass final rinse actuation.
//   References captureSpot (persistent surface).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

rinseBuffer2 = dispense 1 units of PluronicF127_RinseBuffer
rinseStep2   = mix rinseBuffer2 with captureSpot for 2m

dispose rinseStep2

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 10: Fluorescence detection.
//   Device (still positioned on the multiwell plate) is
//   inserted into the PheraStar multiwell plate reader
//   (BMG Labtech). The plate reader reads fluorescence
//   from the FITC-labeled sandwich complex bound to the
//   capture antibody spot on the ITO top plate.
//   Settings: lambda_ex = 485 nm, lambda_em = 520 nm,
//   focal height = 12.1 mm.
//   immunoassayDetection module abstracts the plate reader
//   fluorescence measurement step.
//   After detection, the Cytop/ITO cover plate is discarded;
//   the patterned bottom plate is re-used for subsequent assays.
//   NOTE: detect runs on rinseStep2 as the last active droplet
//   on the device at the time of plate reader insertion.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

assaySignal = detect immunoassayDetection on rinseStep2 for 30s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Assay QC gate
// Pass (BB2): assaySignal > 0 -> FITC fluorescence detected
//             above background; analyte present in sample.
//             Dispose final droplet; assay valid.
// Fail (BB3): assaySignal <= 0 -> no fluorescence above
//             background; analyte absent or assay failed.
//             Drain final droplet.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (assaySignal > 0) {
    dispose rinseStep2
} else {
    drain rinseStep2
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose the assay QC signal. Experiment ends.
// Top plate (Cytop/ITO with capture spot) is discarded.
// Bottom plate is retained for re-use in subsequent assays.
// ============================================================

dispose assaySignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]
